From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ludovic Henry <git@ludovic.dev>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] castxml: scope the _Float128 workaround to the castxml-cc-gnu
 probe, not CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [riscv64 CastXML/Clang 18.1.3 toolchain gap; not relevant to other platforms]

build-itk.yml forces `#define _Float128 long double` via `-include` into the
global CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS, guarded to `#ifdef __clang__` so it only affects the
CastXML/Clang toolchain this -include was written for, not GCC's real build
of ITK's own C++ library sources (which also reads CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS directly
and collides with glibc/libstdc++'s own distinct template specializations
for _Float128 vs long double under GCC).

That guard broke the castxml-cc-gnu probe this same flags string also feeds:
this macro's own `set(_castxml_cc_flags ${CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS})` forwards it
into `--castxml-cc-gnu ("${CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER}" ${_castxml_cc_flags})`, and
CastXML's own detectCC_GNU() (src/Detect.cxx) runs exactly that compiler and
flags array - appending only `-E -dM -v <empty file>` - through the REAL
GNU compiler to dump its predefined macros, never through Clang. GCC never
defines __clang__, so the probe's macro dump never captures the _Float128
override, and castxml's own internal Clang then parses the real wrapping
input files (confirmed on ITKCommonBase.cxx) using that macro set, where
_Float128 is still undefined - reproducing the exact pre-workaround error
("unknown type name '_Float128'" in bits/mathcalls.h) a later commit on
this same branch already fixed once, this time via the fix meant to guard
against the GCC collision instead.

Move the -include out of the string GCC's real library build consumes
(CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS, left alone in the workflow) and into castxml's own
_castxml_cc_flags specifically, right where it is built up, guarded on the
workaround header file actually existing (so an unrelated platform/build
calling this same macro is unaffected either way). This reaches both the
GCC-run probe (whose dumped macros castxml's own internal Clang then
parses the real file with) and, if castxml ever runs its internal Clang
directly against this flags string instead, that path too - without ever
reaching the real GCC compile of ITK's own .cxx sources, so the header's
#define no longer needs the __clang__ guard that defeated the probe.
---
 Wrapping/macro_files/itk_auto_load_submodules.cmake | 12 ++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+)

diff --git a/Wrapping/macro_files/itk_auto_load_submodules.cmake b/Wrapping/macro_files/itk_auto_load_submodules.cmake
index 0bb618f..4427bc1 100644
--- a/Wrapping/macro_files/itk_auto_load_submodules.cmake
+++ b/Wrapping/macro_files/itk_auto_load_submodules.cmake
@@ -19,6 +19,18 @@ function(generate_castxml_commandline_flags)

   # Avoid missing omp.h include
   set(_castxml_cc_flags ${CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS})
+  # riscv64: this image's glibc declares _Float128 math functions unconditionally once
+  # it sees a Clang new enough, but the CastXML/Clang 18.1.3 toolchain this castxml-cc-gnu
+  # probe (and castxml's own internal parse, which configures itself from this probe's
+  # output) ends up invoking does not recognize _Float128 as a type on riscv64. Force-
+  # including a long-double substitute here, rather than into CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS itself,
+  # keeps it out of GCC's real build of ITK's own C++ library sources, which reads
+  # CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS directly and whose riscv64 glibc/libstdc++ headers carry separate
+  # template specializations keyed on _Float128 and on long double (e.g. __iseqsig_type,
+  # numeric_limits) that the same substitution would collapse into one and break.
+  if(EXISTS "/work/float128-workaround.h")
+    set(_castxml_cc_flags "${_castxml_cc_flags} -include /work/float128-workaround.h")
+  endif()
   if(CMAKE_CXX_EXTENSIONS)
     set(_castxml_cc_flags "${_castxml_cc_flags} ${CMAKE_CXX${CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD}_EXTENSION_COMPILE_OPTION}")
   else()
--
